The volume of water in the tank remains constant at $V=100$ gallons because the inflow rate ($2 \text{ gal/min}$) equals the outflow rate ($2 \text{ gal/min}$).

The rate of change of salt is given by:
$$\frac{dQ}{dt} = (\text{Rate of salt in}) - (\text{Rate of salt out})$$

Rate of salt in: (Concentration in) $\times$ (Flow rate in)
$$\text{Rate in} = \left( \frac{1}{4}\left(1+\frac{1}{2} \sin t\right) \frac{\mathrm{oz}}{\mathrm{gal}} \right) \times \left( 2 \frac{\mathrm{gal}}{\mathrm{min}} \right) = \frac{1}{2}\left(1+\frac{1}{2} \sin t\right) = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{4} \sin t$$

Rate of salt out: (Concentration in tank) $\times$ (Flow rate out)
The concentration in the tank at time $t$ is $C(t) = \frac{Q(t)}{V} = \frac{Q(t)}{100}$.
$$\text{Rate out} = \left( \frac{Q(t)}{100} \frac{\mathrm{oz}}{\mathrm{gal}} \right) \times \left( 2 \frac{\mathrm{gal}}{\mathrm{min}} \right) = \frac{2 Q(t)}{100} = \frac{Q(t)}{50}$$

So the differential equation is:
$$\frac{dQ}{dt} = \left( \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{4} \sin t \right) - \frac{Q}{50}$$
$$\frac{dQ}{dt} + \frac{1}{50} Q = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{4} \sin t$$

This is a first-order linear ODE. The long-time behavior is the particular solution $Q_p(t)$ which corresponds to the steady-state (or long-time) behavior.
